From 1e379f96e3e19c8ecb27b437162ad85a50fc3525 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 04:53:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Boundary conditions independent of the unknowns added to the
 residual

The contribution to the residual of some boundary conditions is a vector
independent of the unknowns multiplied by a scalar depending on time only,
their contribution to the tangent operator being null. Such a boundary
condition now reports it (new method `isIndependentOfTheUnknowns` of
`AbstractBoundaryCondition`) and adds its contribution to a residual
(`addContributionToTheResidual`). The imposed pressure and the heat source
are such boundary conditions: their contributions are assembled once, for
a unit value, by the linear form integrators used by the prediction, then
scaled by the value at the end of the time step, on the device if a
device is enabled.

With the inner forces computed by kernels, such a boundary condition is no
longer an integrator of the non linear form: its contribution is added to
the inner forces computed by kernels before the transfer to the true
degrees of freedom. If the non linear form contains no other integrator
than the one of mfem-mgis, the residual is no longer assembled element by
element on the host, the tangent operator is assembled by kernels without
the assembly of MFEM, and it can be applied without assembly.

Tests: BatchIntegrationTest compares to the integration point by point
the imposed pressure added to the inner forces computed by kernels, with
the tangent operator assembled by kernels or applied without assembly,
also with the FBar formulation and two materials, the imposed pressure as
an integrator of the non linear form with the inner forces computed
element by element, and springs depending on the unknowns, an integrator
of the non linear form besides the one of mfem-mgis; the refusal of the
tangent operator applied without assembly is tested with these springs.
HeatTransferKernelsTest: cases with a heat source, stationary and
transient.
